\section{Reparameterization}{

A random-effect block has a FUNNEL in its centered joint posterior:
the width of the prior on \code{b} is a standard deviation that is being
sampled at the same time, so the region NUTS must explore narrows as
that standard deviation shrinks, and one step size cannot fit both
ends of it.

\code{reparameterize = TRUE} (the default) samples \code{z ~ N(0, I)} instead
and computes \verb{b = L(theta) z} on the tape, with \code{L} the block's own
Cholesky factor, which is brms's construction. Each draw is mapped back
through ITS OWN \code{theta}, so the \code{b[i]} columns of the draws matrix
hold the same quantity in the same order under the same names as
\code{reparameterize = FALSE} gives, and every method downstream
(\code{\link[=posterior_epred]{posterior_epred()}}, \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}}, \code{\link[=log_lik]{log_lik()}}, \code{\link[=loo]{loo()}},
\code{\link[=conditional_effects]{conditional_effects()}}, \code{\link[=hypothesis]{hypothesis()}}) reads them without knowing
which route produced them. Only the \code{stanfit} inside the object
carries \code{z}.

Priors are unaffected. They are declared on the OUTER parameters
(\code{beta}, \code{theta}, \code{betad}), and \code{theta} means the same thing on both
routes; nothing prior-able is reparameterized. What priors DO decide
is which blocks are eligible, below.

\emph{Which blocks, and why not all of them.} A block is non-centered when
two things hold, and they are the same thing twice: every parameter it
has is a standard deviation or a correlation with a Cholesky factor
registered for its structure, and every one of those parameters
carries a PRIOR. Both are about not handing the sampler a direction
it can run away in.
Non-centering gives NUTS the run of \code{theta}'s whole range, and the
centered funnel is what was keeping a chain out of the parts of that
range where the posterior is flat or improper. Removing the funnel
without closing those off first trades a slow chain for a wrong one.

In practice that means the FORMULA interface, whose default priors
cover every standard deviation and every correlation, non-centers
\code{(1 | g)} and any block written one term at a time, \code{diag()} and
\code{homdiag()} blocks, \code{\link[mgcv:s]{mgcv::s()}} smooths, \code{equalto()}, \code{gr(cov = )},
and the CORRELATED blocks \code{(x | g)}, \code{cs()}, \code{ar1()} and \code{hetar1()}.
A \verb{k =} Hilbert-space \code{\link[brms:gp]{gp()}} block stays
centered on the formula route even though its factor is diagonal:
its LENGTHSCALES share the block's \code{theta}, the default priors cover
only standard deviations, and the gate wants every parameter of a
block priored. Prior the whole block by hand
(\code{set_prior(class = "theta")}) to non-center it.
\code{rr()} is already non-centered by
construction, since its own coefficients are the standard normal
factors. A fitted model sampled with \code{frm_sample(fit)} has flat
priors by design (it is a diagnostic; see above), so it stays
centered unless you give its variance parameters a prior.

The call \code{message()}s every block it left centered, with the reason.
A model made only of those samples centered throughout and says so
rather than failing. The reasons:
\itemize{
\item A FLAT PRIOR on the block's standard deviation. Send \code{sd} down with
\code{z} where it is: \verb{b = sd z} goes to zero, the likelihood settles on
the model without that random effect, and the density stops
changing: a flat tail with nothing to stop a chain in it. Measured
on a six-group random-intercept model, one chain of 2000, three
seeds: from the fit, with flat priors, the non-centered chain walks
\code{theta} to -1e15 at a bulk-ESS of 1; from the formula, where the
default \code{student_t(3, 0, s)} makes that tail integrable, 174 to 284
against 3 to 48 centered.
\item A FLAT PRIOR on the block's CORRELATION, which is the same
argument: flat on frmtmb's unbounded correlation parameter is
\code{(1 - rho^2)^-3/2}, improper, with all its mass at \verb{|rho| = 1}.
Before 0.39 that kept every correlated block centered, because no
proper correlation prior existed to close the tail; \code{lkj(eta)}
now does, and the formula route sets \code{lkj(1)} by default, so
\code{(Days | Subject)}, \code{cs()}, \code{ar1()} and \code{hetar1()} non-center
there like any other block. On the FIT route they still do not:
its priors are flat by design.
\item A Student-t latent (\code{gr(dist = "student")}): a scale mixture, not a
linear factor.
\item \code{\link[brms:car]{car()}}, \code{spde()} and \code{gr(prec = )}: sparse precisions whose factor
is dense.
\item The exact \code{\link[brms:gp]{gp()}} and the spatial covariances (\code{ou}, \code{exp}, \code{gau},
\code{mat}): a full factorization per gradient evaluation.
\item \code{toep()}: not positive definite everywhere in its parameterization,
so \verb{b = L z} is not a bijection there.
}

\emph{What it is worth.} One chain of 2000 iterations, three seeds
(dev/benchmarks.md). Where each group's own data say little (the
regime the funnel lives in) it is decisive: 80 groups of 2 binary
observations run at a min-ESS of 236 against 5 centered, 55 times the
effective draws per second. Where the groups are informative it is a
wash: the \code{epilepsy} GLMM and an uncorrelated \code{sleepstudy} are within
noise of the centered chain either way. It is not a blanket speed-up,
and the blocks it declines to touch are the ones where it would have
done harm.

\code{laplace = TRUE} integrates the random effects out, which removes the
funnel by itself, so that route ignores \code{reparameterize} entirely.

The ML fit is untouched either way. The Laplace approximation
integrates \code{b} out, and that integral is invariant under a linear
change of the integrated variable, so \code{frm()} has nothing to change
and no fitted object ever carries a non-centered tape.
}

\section{Default priors}{

The formula interface defaults to brms 2.23's own weakly-informative
priors, read off \code{brms::default_prior()} on matched models. Write
\verb{y*} for the response transformed by the \code{mu} link and
\verb{s = max(2.5, round(mad(y*), 1))}:\tabular{lll}{
   class \tab default \tab scale \cr
   \code{b} (slopes) \tab flat \tab - \cr
   \code{Intercept} \tab \verb{student_t(3, round(median(y*), 1), s)} \tab link \cr
   \code{sd} \tab \code{student_t(3, 0, s)} \tab natural sd, log-Jacobian applied \cr
   \code{cor} \tab \code{lkj(1)} \tab correlation matrix, Jacobian applied \cr
   \code{sigma} (intercept only) \tab \code{student_t(3, 0, s)} \tab natural \cr
   \code{sigma} (with a predictor) \tab \code{student_t(3, 0, 2.5)} \tab log \cr
}


The link is transformed only for \code{identity}, \code{log}, \code{inverse},
\code{sqrt} and \code{1/mu^2} - brms's own list - with a log-scale family
(\code{\link[=lognormal]{lognormal()}} and its relatives, whose \code{mu} link is spelled
\code{identity} but whose response is logged inside the density) counted
as \code{log}. Under any other link (\code{logit}, \code{cloglog}, ...), for a
response that was a factor, and for a family whose parameters run
over categories (ordinal, categorical, multinomial), the location is
0 and the scale 2.5 - which is what brms reports for a bernoulli
model. Under a \code{log}, \code{inverse} or \code{1/mu^2} link the ZEROS of the
response are replaced by \code{0.1} before the transform, element by
element, exactly as brms does; the non-zero values are left alone,
so a count response with median 1 keeps \code{log(1) = 0} as its
location.

The call \code{message()}s the priors it chose, one compact line per
class, AND one line per slot it deliberately left flat, so a model
that gets few defaults - or none - says so rather than looking flat
by accident. Wrap it in \code{suppressMessages()} to silence that.
\code{prior_summary()} on the returned draws reproduces the chosen priors
exactly.

\emph{What is deliberately NOT matched.} Each of these is named in the
message whenever the model has one.
\itemize{
\item A \code{toep()} correlation stays flat. Its banded parameterization is
not positive definite everywhere, so there is no correlation matrix
over its whole parameter space for an LKJ density to be about.
Every other correlated structure gets \code{lkj(1)}, brms's own default
(see \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}} for what the density is on frmtmb's
parameters).
\item ORDINAL THRESHOLDS stay flat. brms priors them
\code{student_t(3, 0, 2.5)} under its \code{Intercept} class; frmtmb keeps
them in the \code{thres} extra-parameter vector, which is not a design
column and which \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}}'s class vocabulary
(\code{b}/\code{Intercept}/\code{sd}/\code{theta}) cannot address. An ordinal model
still gets its \code{sd} defaults, and the message names the gap.
\item The \code{shape}, \code{phi} and \code{nu} dispersion parameters stay flat: brms
gives them gamma and inverse-gamma defaults, which \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}}
does not carry.
\item MULTIVARIATE models get no defaults at all, because \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}}
cannot address one response of several.
}

\emph{Overriding and opting out.} A \code{set_prior()} specification takes over
the classes it names and leaves the other defaults in place, which is
brms's partial-override rule; a named list of prior objects takes
over exactly the internal parameters it names. \code{priors = "flat"}
turns the defaults off entirely and samples the likelihood, which
warns when the model has variance components: their flat-prior
posteriors need not be proper, and neither the chains nor Rhat can
see that.
}

\section{Multimodal posteriors}{

For \code{\link[=mixture]{mixture()}} fits the posterior is multimodal by construction
(label switching at minimum). Mode-centered inits, jittered or
not, leave every chain in one symmetry branch, so Rhat cannot
flag the others; use \code{init = "random"} there and inspect chains
individually.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("tmbstan", quietly = TRUE) &&
    requireNamespace("rstan", quietly = TRUE)) {
set.seed(9)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(80), g = factor(rep(1:8, 10)))
dd$y <- rnorm(80, 1 + 0.5 * dd$x + rnorm(8, 0, 0.5)[dd$g], 1)
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd)
ds <- frm_sample(fit, chains = 1, iter = 500, refresh = 0)
summary(ds)
fixef(ds)
hypothesis(ds, "sd_g__Intercept^2 / (sd_g__Intercept^2 + sigma^2)")

# the same model sampled straight from the formula, with no ML fit.
# It reports the brms default priors it chose, and prior_summary()
# gives them back.
ds2 <- frm_sample(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)), data = dd, family = gaussian(),
                  chains = 1, iter = 500, refresh = 0)
prior_summary(ds2)
fixef(ds2)

# a set_prior() specification takes over the classes it names and
# leaves the rest of the defaults alone
ds3 <- frm_sample(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)), data = dd, family = gaussian(),
                  chains = 1, iter = 500, refresh = 0,
                  priors = set_prior("exponential(1)", class = "sd"))
prior_summary(ds3)
}
}
}
